            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the rotation that turns one set of points (or directions) onto another as closely as possible - the Kabsch method - and gives that rotation, the turned points and how well they fit.\n\nUse it to calibrate: line a sensor's or tracker's frame up with the room from a few matching points. To align a skeleton or a captured pose with a reference whatever way it faces. To compare two poses after taking their facing out, or to measure how much a rigid group of markers has turned between two moments.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.align_vectors\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nfrom:\nThe points to be turned, one row of x y z each. Every input is fitted and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nonto:\nWhere they should end up, the same number of rows, held. Row n of from is matched with row n of onto.\n\ntreat as:\nAn option, under the node. points (the default): each set is first centred on its own middle, so the fit finds the turn of the shape whatever its position - the aligned output is moved onto the target too. directions: the rows are directions from the origin (unit vectors, axes) and are not centred.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotation:\nThe best rotation, as w x y z.\n\naligned:\nThe from points after the turn (and, for points, the move).\n\nerror:\nHow far off the fit is: the square root of the summed squared misses. 0 is a perfect fit. Sent first, then aligned, then rotation.\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour points, and the same four turned 30 degrees about y and moved 5 along x. The rotation found is 0.9659 0 0.2588 0 - exactly 30 degrees about y - and the error is 0. A metro re-sends the points twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\ntracker_align lines up a tracker with a body. scipy.procrustes compares shapes while also ignoring size. scipy.rotation_apply turns points by a rotation.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Finds the rotation that turns one set of points (or directions) onto another as closely as possible - the Kabsch method - and gives that rotation, the turned points and how well they fit.\n\nUse it to calibrate: line a sensor's or tracker's frame up with the room from a few matching points. To align a skeleton or a captured pose with a reference whatever way it faces. To compare two poses after taking their facing out, or to measure how much a rigid group of markers has turned between two moments.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.align_vectors\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nfrom:\nThe points to be turned, one row of x y z each. Every input is fitted and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nonto:\nWhere they should end up, the same number of rows, held. Row n of from is matched with row n of onto.\n\ntreat as:\nAn option, under the node. points (the default): each set is first centred on its own middle, so the fit finds the turn of the shape whatever its position - the aligned output is moved onto the target too. directions: the rows are directions from the origin (unit vectors, axes) and are not centred.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotation:\nThe best rotation, as w x y z.\n\naligned:\nThe from points after the turn (and, for points, the move).\n\nerror:\nHow far off the fit is: the square root of the summed squared misses. 0 is a perfect fit. Sent first, then aligned, then rotation.\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour points, and the same four turned 30 degrees about y and moved 5 along x. The rotation found is 0.9659 0 0.2588 0 - exactly 30 degrees about y - and the error is 0. A metro re-sends the points twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\ntracker_align lines up a tracker with a body. scipy.procrustes compares shapes while also ignoring size. scipy.rotation_apply turns points by a rotation."
        },
